    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d307">

                <pb facs="0253" n="241" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg241"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.Consultation(2)/433: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="73" xml:id="d307-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-07-24T15:00:00-03:00">July 24, 1940—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>366. Nabuco<anchor n="74" xml:id="d307-f2"/> reports there is talk in some
                    quarters Habana that “autonomy should be conceded existing colonies in America”.
                    Aranha <pb facs="0254" n="242" xml:id="pg242"/> says that he disagrees with
                    this. He supports the Department’s position regarding the colonies.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d307-f1" n="73" type="source" xml:id="d307-f1-content">
                <p>This message was repeated to the American Delegate at Habana in Department’s
                    telegram No. 26, July 25, 2 p.m.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d307-f2" n="74" xml:id="d307-f2-content">
                <p>Mauricio Nabuco, personal representative of the Brazilian Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs at the Second Meeting of the Ministers of Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
